## Context

The deferred close/archive redesign (STATE.md, "Premature / unproven" section,
commits 3451c01 / dca943c) couples two changes: (1) `erg close` auto-moves the
ticket into `tickets/closed/`, killing the closed-but-unarchived limbo; (2)
`erg archive` is repurposed to an epoch-rollover sweep (`closed/*.erg →
closed/<epoch>/`). The combined design was flagged breaking and parked.

This ticket is **phase 1 only**, and the analysis below shows it is **not
breaking** — contrary to the STATE note, which lumped the breaking bits
(repurposing `archive`, dropping the nag hook, `next-id` epoch logic) with the
benign one. Those all stay in **phase 2 (epoch rollover, still deferred)**.

The decoupling: `erg close` gains the move; `erg archive` and the pre-push nag
hook are left exactly as shipped. `archive` remains the safety-net sweep for
*hand-closed* tickets (a `Closed:` header added by hand, without `erg close`).
Net effect of phase 1: `close` becomes one-step; nothing's semantics change;
`archive` usually finds nothing because `close` already moved the file.

## Relevant files

- `src/go/close.go` — `cmdClose` today writes the `Closed:` header
  (`insertClosedHeader`), appends the log line, then strips `Blocked-by:` refs
  from OPEN dependents (`removeBlockedByRef`, step 3). It does NOT move the file.
  Add the move after step 3.
- `src/go/archive.go` — `cmdArchive` owns the move primitive today: `MkdirAll`
  `closed/`, `os.Stat` collision check, `os.Rename` (lines ~146-164), guarded by
  a reverse `blockedBy` index built from OPEN tickets only (lines ~70-82). Factor
  the bare move (MkdirAll + collision-check + Rename) into a shared helper so both
  `close` and (phase 2) the epoch sweep reuse one atomic-move path.
- `src/go/nextid.go` — `maxIDInDir` uses a recursive `filepath.WalkDir`, so
  tickets under `closed/` already count as reserved IDs. LEAVE UNTOUCHED in
  phase 1 (see Invariants). This is what guarantees auto-archiving does not
  recycle an ID.
- `src/go/erg.go` — `IsClosed` / `pathIsClosed`: a `closed` path segment at ANY
  depth reads closed, so the disjunctive read criterion already tolerates the
  phase-2 `closed/<epoch>/` nesting. Do not tighten it.
- `tickets/erg` — committed bootstrap binary; must be rebuilt (`make
  update-bootstrap-binary`, verify with `make verify`).
- `erg spec` §5 (close = 3-step; archive = mover) — text needs a phase-1 update.

## Actions

1. Extract the move primitive from `cmdArchive` into a shared helper, e.g.
   `moveTicketToClosed(ticketDir string, t *Erg) error` (MkdirAll `closed/`,
   `os.Stat` destination collision check, `os.Rename`). Have `cmdArchive` call it.
2. In `cmdClose`, after step 3 (Blocked-by removal), call the helper to move the
   just-closed ticket into `tickets/closed/`. Ordering: header-write at the
   original top-level path → step 3 → move. (close strips all OPEN dependents'
   refs in step 3, so the move is never blocked — closed dependents keep their
   historical refs but are not blockers.)
3. Keep the `CLOSED (already)` idempotency branch BEFORE the move: an
   already-closed ticket (incl. a hand-closed one still at top-level) is not
   moved by `close` — `archive` sweeps those. Preserves current idempotency.
4. Update `helpClose` (the 3-step description) to a 4th step: "moves the closed
   ticket into DIR/closed/". Update `erg spec` §5 accordingly.
5. Rebuild the bootstrap binary: `make update-bootstrap-binary`.

## Forward-compatibility with phase 2 (epoch rollover)

_Note (2026-06-27): phase 2 (epoch rollover) was dropped after review — ID-space
scaling now goes via the path-ref multi-store split (rationale in
pep-erg-v1.md). The four constraints below still stand on their own merit: they
keep phase 1 minimal and non-breaking, independent of any successor._

Phase 1 must not paint phase 2 into a corner. The four load-bearing constraints:

- **Move to `closed/` TOP-LEVEL only**, never a dated subdir. Phase-2 `next-id`
  treats top-level `closed/` as current-epoch (IDs still reserved); the dated
  `closed/<epoch>/` it adds is the frozen, recyclable tier.
- **Shared move helper** (Action 1) is the primitive phase 2's `closed/*.erg →
  closed/<epoch>/` sweep reuses — don't inline the rename into `close.go`.
- **Do not touch `nextid.go`.** Its recursive walk counts everything under
  `closed/` as reserved — correct for phase 1. Phase 2 is precisely the change
  that narrows the walk to skip `closed/<epoch>/`; pre-empting it here would
  conflict.
- **Do not tighten `pathIsClosed`** — it already matches a `closed` segment at
  any depth, so phase-2 nesting reads closed for free. Keep the read criterion
  disjunctive (Closed: header OR path).

## Test

Red step first (`src/go/close_test.go`): `erg close <ID> <reason>` on a
top-level open ticket must leave the file at `tickets/closed/<ID>-slug.erg`
(not top-level), with the `Closed:` header and the `closed —` log line intact.
Add a second assertion that `next-id` after the close still returns an ID
greater than the just-closed one (ID not recycled — guards the nextid.go
invariant). Keep an idempotency test: a hand-closed top-level ticket is NOT
moved by `erg close` (prints `CLOSED (already)`, stays put for `archive`).

## Verification

- [ ] `erg close <ID> <reason>` moves the ticket into `tickets/closed/`
- [ ] Closed header + `closed —` log line present in the moved file
- [ ] `next-id` does not recycle the closed ticket's ID
- [ ] Hand-closed top-level ticket left in place by `close`; swept by `archive`
- [ ] `erg archive` and the pre-push nag hook behave exactly as before
- [ ] `make verify` (bootstrap binary byte-matches embedded source)
- [ ] `make check` passes
- [ ] `make regen-assets && git diff --exit-code tickets/` clean (embedded assets)

## Invariants

- `nextid.go` untouched — no ID recycling (recursive walk counts `closed/`).
- The `CLOSED` stdout token is unchanged — `erg-pr-merge` parses it; do not
  rename it to `ARCHIVED` or `CLOSED (archived)`.
- `archive.go` semantics and the `erg install --push-hook` nag are unchanged.
- Existing close tests (header insertion, step-3 Blocked-by removal,
  idempotency) and archive tests stay green.
- No test asserts `closed/` is flat / has no subdirectories (phase 2 nests).
- `erg check` PASS on the store.

## Exit criteria

`erg close` relocates the closed ticket into `tickets/closed/` in addition to
its existing header + log + Blocked-by-strip steps; `archive` and the nag hook
unchanged; the move primitive is shared (not inlined) so phase 2 can reuse it;
`nextid.go` and `pathIsClosed` untouched; help text + spec updated; bootstrap
binary rebuilt; `make check` and `make verify` pass.

## Review hardening (red-team panel, 2026-06-27)

Findings to address during implementation:

- **Route the move through a durability-preserving helper, not a bare
  `os.Rename`.** `cmdArchive`'s mover does not fsync; `close` already does a
  fsync'd atomic header write, then this non-durable rename. A crash after the
  rename returns but before the `closed/` directory entry is flushed can lose the
  ticket — a never-lose-data violation. `moveTicketToClosed` must fsync both the
  source and destination parent dirs (mirror `atomicWriteFile`).
- **Make the idempotency branch and ID-resolution move-aware.** A crash mid-
  sequence (header -> Blocked-by sweep -> move) strands a closed ticket at
  top-level, which `erg check` flags as a hard error; re-running `erg close <ID>`
  does NOT repair it — the `CLOSED (already)` branch returns before the move, and
  `resolveTicketByID` globs `DIR/<ID>-*.erg` non-recursively so the ID form
  cannot even find a moved file. Fix: if a ticket is closed-but-top-level, fall
  through to the move; and have ID resolution fall back to `closed/` so
  `erg close <ID>` stays idempotent (and findable) after archiving.
- **Helper takes two inputs.** Source is `t.Path`; destination base is
  `ticketDir` (dest = `filepath.Join(ticketDir, "closed", t.Filename())`), since
  for the FILE form `t.Path` need not be a child of `ticketDir`. The collision
  guard stays a non-atomic `os.Stat`-then-`Rename` (parity with archive); leave
  the open-only `blockedBy` guard at archive's call site (close pre-clears open
  dependents in step 3, so its move is never blocked).
- **Spec citation fix.** This ticket says "spec §5"; the spec has no numbered
  sections — the relevant header is `### Closed / not-closed criterion` (and
  `### ID assignment`). Cite the named `###` headers when updating the spec.
